# Candiquator — Complete Recreation Spec

A desktop candy-drop math puzzle written as a single Python 3 file using pygame.
Window title: Candiquator
Source filename typically: Candiquator.py
Config file: ~/.candiquator.json

The document below is enough to rebuild the game from scratch.

------------------------------------------------------------
1. Purpose
------------------------------------------------------------
The player clears numbered candies from a 10×10 grid by forming a correct
arithmetic equation with three candies:

    first selected value  OPERATOR  second selected value  =  third selected value

Alternatively the player may select two candies, type the result, and press
Equals. The typed result must exist as a number on some other candy; that
candy is the third term and is removed too.

Goal of each ROUND: remove 50 candies (CLEAR_GOAL = 50).
A LEVEL is one full set of four rounds in this order:
    Addition → Subtraction → Multiplication → Division
Then the level number increases and the cycle repeats.

Escape quits. Window is resizable and its size/position are saved and restored.

------------------------------------------------------------
2. Command line
------------------------------------------------------------
    python3 Candiquator.py
    python3 Candiquator.py LEVEL

LEVEL is an integer. Default is 1. Clamp to 1..4.
If the argument is not an integer, print usage and exit.

    Usage: python3 Candiquator.py [level]
    Level default is 1. Valid levels: 1-4

------------------------------------------------------------
3. Dependencies and init
------------------------------------------------------------
Standard library: json, math, os, random, sys, array
Third party: pygame

Mixer must be pre-initialized before pygame.init():
    pygame.mixer.pre_init(22050, -16, 1, 512)
Then pygame.init().

Sounds are synthesized in memory (no audio files).

------------------------------------------------------------
4. Constants
------------------------------------------------------------
ROWS = 10
COLS = 10
TEXT_TRIES_START = 10
CLEAR_GOAL = 50
FALL_MS_PER_CELL = 300.0   # one grid cell of falling takes 300 milliseconds

LEVELS = {
    1: {"add": 20,   "mul": 50,   "matches": 20, "seconds": 10 * 60},
    2: {"add": 50,   "mul": 100,  "matches": 15, "seconds":  8 * 60},
    3: {"add": 100,  "mul": 300,  "matches": 10, "seconds":  5 * 60},
    4: {"add": 500,  "mul": 1024, "matches":  5, "seconds":  4 * 60},
}

Meaning of fields:
    add      = maximum value used for addition and subtraction (operands and result)
    mul      = maximum value used for multiplication and division
    matches  = minimum number of valid 3-candy equations that must exist on the board
    seconds  = time limit for ONE round (one operator), not the whole level

Operators, in order:
    ("+",  lambda a, b: a + b,                         "Addition")
    ("-",  lambda a, b: a - b,                         "Subtraction")
    ("x",  lambda a, b: a * b,                         "Multiplication")
    ("/",  lambda a, b: a // b if b and a % b == 0 else None, "Division")

Division is integer-only. If b == 0 or a is not divisible by b, the result is None (invalid).

Candy color palette (fill, highlight, edge), six kinds:
    strawberry  (255,77,109)  (255,143,163)  (139,0,32)
    blueberry   (76,201,240)  (144,224,239)  (0,119,182)
    lime        (128,185,24)  (181,228,140)  (36,85,1)
    lemon       (255,183,3)   (255,224,138)  (179,107,0)
    grape       (155,93,229)  (199,125,255)  (90,24,154)
    cherry      (247,37,133)  (255,153,200)  (157,2,8)

Shapes: "circle", "oval", "lifesaver", "pretzel"
Stripe styles: None, "horiz", "diag", "cross"

Background color: (20, 12, 34)
Empty well color: (30, 18, 51)
HUD text: (255, 230, 109)
Hint text: (189, 224, 254)
Message text: (205, 180, 219)

------------------------------------------------------------
5. Saved window geometry
------------------------------------------------------------
JSON file ~/.candiquator.json
Keys: w, h, x, y  (integers)

On launch:
    default size 920×780, minimum 640×520
    if x,y present, set SDL2 window position via pygame._sdl2.video.Window.from_display_module()
    wrap in try/except; if SDL2 Window is unavailable, ignore position

On resize, close, or Escape:
    save current size and (if available) position

Window flag: pygame.RESIZABLE

------------------------------------------------------------
6. Data types
------------------------------------------------------------
Candy:
    kind            int 0..5  (palette index / “color”)
    value           int       (printed on the candy)
    bomb            bool      (cherry bomb)
    shape           one of SHAPES
    bow             bool      ~28%
    stripes         one of STRIPE_STYLES
    swirl           bool      ~22%
    dots            bool      ~28%
    translucent     bool      ~20%
    dot_color       RGB from palette fill colors
    swirl_color     RGB from palette highlight colors
    visual_y        float or None  (animated row; target is the grid row)
    sparkle         unused float (sparkles are separate objects)

If bomb is True, force:
    shape = "circle", no bow, no stripes, no swirl, no dots, not translucent
    colors when drawn: fill (40,40,48), highlight (220,50,50), edge (10,10,10)

Particle (explosion / pop debris):
    x, y, vx, vy, life (18..36 frames), color, radius 2..6
    gravity: vy += 0.35 each frame

Sparkle (twinkle on a candy):
    grid r,c
    ang in 0..2π, rad 0.15..0.7 (fraction of candy radius)
    life 16..28 frames, max_life = life, size 2..5
    drawn as a small bright circle plus a 4-pixel plus-sign
    removed when life hits 0 or the candy at r,c is gone

------------------------------------------------------------
7. Game state
------------------------------------------------------------
grid[r][c]          Candy or None
op_i                0..3 index into OPS
level               1..4
score               int
tries               text-box uses remaining (start 10)
cleared             candies removed this round (0..50)
selected            list of (r,c), max 3
over                bool
msg                 status string
typed               text in the result field
entry_focus         bool
particles, sparkles
fuse_tick           int, for bomb fuse flicker and occasional fuse sound
round_end           pygame.time.get_ticks() + seconds*1000 at start of each round
clock               60 FPS

rules() returns LEVELS[level] (level 4 table if somehow higher).
cap() is rules["add"] when operator is + or -, else rules["mul"].
min_matches() is rules["matches"].

------------------------------------------------------------
8. Number generation
------------------------------------------------------------
make_triple() plants a valid (a, b, result) for the current operator,
all values within cap:

    Addition:      a in 1..cap-1, b in 1..cap-a, result = a+b
    Subtraction:   result c in 1..cap-1, b in 1..cap-c, a = b+c
                   so a - b = c, all positive and <= cap
    Multiplication: a in 1..(floor(sqrt(cap))+1), b in 1..(cap//a), result = a*b
    Division:      b in 1..max(1, floor(sqrt(cap))),
                   c in 1..max(1, cap//b),
                   a = b*c, so a / b = c exactly

rand_val() = random integer 1..cap inclusive.

spawn(bomb=False, kind=None, value=None, from_above=False):
    random kind if None, random value if None
    if from_above: visual_y = -1.5 - random()*3

------------------------------------------------------------
9. Matches (valid equations on the board)
------------------------------------------------------------
A MATCH is a set of three distinct occupied cells {P, Q, R} such that
some ordering of two of them as operands produces the third cell’s value
under the current operator.

Count unique frozensets of three cells, not ordered pairs.

Algorithm:
    bucket cells by value
    for each unordered pair of distinct cells (i < j):
        try both operand orders (a,b) and (b,a)
        res = compute(a,b)
        if res is None: skip
        for each other cell whose value == res:
            add frozenset of the three positions

has_move() is count_matches() > 0.

plant_triple(): pick 3 random occupied cells, overwrite their values with
a fresh make_triple().

ensure_matches(target=None):
    need = min_matches() if target is None else target
    while count_matches() < need and fewer than 80 attempts:
        plant_triple()

This MUST run:
    after building a new board
    after every collapse-and-refill (especially Level 4: always at least 5)

HUD may show the live match count.

------------------------------------------------------------
10. Board setup and refill
------------------------------------------------------------
new_board():
    fill 10×10 with spawned candies
    ~4% are bombs
    visual_y starts at row - ROWS - random*2 so they fall in from above
    ensure_matches()
    cleared = 0, selected = [], over = False, sparkles cleared
    start_timer()
    message describes level, operator, goal 50, and minimum matches

collapse_and_refill() per column:
    compact existing candies toward the BOTTOM (gravity)
    spawn `missing` new candies at the TOP
    new candies: ~5% bombs, visual_y = -1 - i - random so they fall
    then ensure_matches()

drop_bomb_from_sky():
    pick a random column
    create a bomb with visual_y = -2
    place in first empty cell of that column, else replace row 0
    append "  A cherry bomb drops!" to msg

Unlimited candies: the board is always refilled to 10×10 after removals.

------------------------------------------------------------
11. Falling animation
------------------------------------------------------------
Each frame:
    step = dt_ms / FALL_MS_PER_CELL
    if visual_y is None: visual_y = grid row
    if visual_y < target row: visual_y = min(target, visual_y + step)
    else visual_y = target

Draw the candy at interpolated row visual_y, not at the discrete grid row.
Click detection still uses the discrete grid cell under the mouse
(wells stay fixed; only sprites fall).

------------------------------------------------------------
12. Player input
------------------------------------------------------------
Mouse left click:
    Result text box rect  → focus entry
    "=" button            → use_text()
    "Clear pick" button   → selected = []
    candy well            → toggle that cell in selected
        if already selected: deselect
        if already 3 selected: clear selection then add this one
        after adding, if len(selected)==3: try_triple(selected)

Keyboard:
    Escape     save geometry, quit
    Return     use_text()
    Backspace  delete last typed char
    '-'        allowed only as first character of typed
    digits     append if length < 8; set entry_focus

Ignore input when over == True except Escape / quit.

try_triple(cells):
    a, b, c = values of the three cells in selection order
    res = compute(a,b)
    if res is None or res != c:
        message "Nope: a OP b != c", play bad sound, clear selection
        return
    same_color = all three kind values equal
    resolve(cells, same_color)

use_text():
    requires tries > 0 and exactly 2 selected and typed int
    decrement tries first after a parsed integer (including wrong answers)
    if compute(a,b) != typed: bad sound, check_over
    else find a third candy whose value == typed, not one of the two
        prefer a third candy whose kind equals both selected kinds
    if no such candy: message that the result is not on the board, check_over
    else resolve three cells, same_color if all three kinds match

------------------------------------------------------------
13. Resolve / scoring / bombs
------------------------------------------------------------
explode_around(cells):
    for each selected cell that is a bomb:
        add all occupied cells in the 3×3 neighborhood (including itself)
        spawn ~28 particles at its center (oranges, yellows, darks)
    if any bomb neighborhood collected: play soft boom
    return the extra cell set

resolve(cells, same_color):
    doomed = selected cells ∪ explode_around extras
    for each doomed occupied cell:
        points += abs(value)
        spawn ~10 particles in that candy’s color
        set grid cell to None
        n += 1
    cleared += n
    score += points * (2 if same_color else 1) * level
    if same_color: tries += 1
    selected = []
    message reports n removed, cleared/50, points, optional same-color note
    play ok + pop sounds
    collapse_and_refill()
    if same_color: drop_bomb_from_sky()
    elif random() < 0.12: drop_bomb_from_sky()   # bombs also arrive on their own
    if cleared >= 50: advance()
    else check_over()

advance():
    op_i = (op_i + 1) % 4
    if op_i wrapped to 0 (finished Division):
        level = min(level + 1, 4)
        tries += 5
    else:
        tries += 3
    new_board()   # resets cleared and timer for the new operator

check_over():
    if time remaining <= 0: over, "Time's up! ..."
    elif has_move(): return
    elif tries > 0: hint to use the text box
    else: over, "Game over! ..."

Time is checked every frame in animate() as well.

------------------------------------------------------------
14. Drawing
------------------------------------------------------------
Layout:
    top HUD band 56 px, bottom UI band 92 px, pad 10
    cell width  = (window_w - 2*pad) / 10
    cell height = (window_h - top - bottom - pad) / 10
    candy radius m = min(cw, ch) * 0.40
    cell center uses visual row when drawing sprites

HUD line (top):
    Lv {level} {op name} ({symbol})  Score  Tries
    Removed {cleared}/50 ({left} left)  Matches {n}+  Time M:SS

Each well: dark circle.
Then each candy sprite.

Candy sprite is an SRCALPHA surface ~2.6m × 2.8m.

Draw by shape:
    oval       ellipse 2.1m × 1.55m
    lifesaver  filled circle with inner hole colored like the board background
               radius hole = 0.38 m
    pretzel    two thick arched polylines plus a small bottom knot
               a couple of light “salt” dots
    circle     default, also used for bombs

Decorations (skipped on bombs):
    swirl     18-point spiral polyline in swirl_color
    dots      4 polka dots in dot_color
    stripes   white line(s):
                horiz  through center
                diag   corner to corner
                cross  two crossing “gift ribbon” lines (lacey X)
    highlight small lighter circle upper-left
    bow       two red lobes + pink knot sitting on the top edge

Translucent candies: fill alpha 140 instead of 255 (~20% of non-bombs).

Bombs:
    dark body
    brown fuse line up and right
    flickering orange/yellow flame whose size uses sin(fuse_tick * 0.4)

White number centered on the candy, font size shrinks for 3–4 digit values
(needed at level 4, values up to 1024).

Selected candies: white ring outside the sprite, and a gold index 1/2/3
above the candy showing selection order.

Sparkles after candies, particles after sparkles.

Bottom UI:
    result field (placeholder text "result" when empty)
    yellow "=" button
    purple "Clear pick" button
    hint: "Remove 50 candies to advance. Esc quits."
    status message to the right of the buttons

------------------------------------------------------------
15. Sparkle timing
------------------------------------------------------------
Each frame, for every candy, probability 0.003 to spawn a Sparkle at that cell.
Sparkles fade by life/max_life and are drawn in pale yellow-white.

------------------------------------------------------------
16. Sound (synthesized)
------------------------------------------------------------
Sample rate 22050, 16-bit signed mono, array.array("h").

make_tone(freq, ms, vol, decay=True)
    sine wave, envelope (1 - i/n)^1.6 if decay

make_soft_boom(ms=1100, vol=0.18)
    long muted thud: mix of sines at 58, 92, 41 Hz plus low-passed noise
    exponential decay exp(-t * 3.2)
    NOT a short harsh noise burst

Instances:
    snd_pop   660 Hz, 140 ms, vol 0.18
    snd_ok    740 Hz, 220 ms, vol 0.16
    snd_bad   180 Hz, 260 ms, vol 0.16
    snd_boom  soft boom 1100 ms
    snd_fuse  90 Hz, 160 ms, vol 0.06
              played about every 40 frames if any bomb is on the board

------------------------------------------------------------
17. Main loop
------------------------------------------------------------
60 FPS
Handle QUIT, VIDEORESIZE (recreate RESIZABLE display and persist),
MOUSEBUTTONDOWN button 1, KEYDOWN.
Then animate(), draw().

------------------------------------------------------------
18. Look-and-feel / extra rules already decided
------------------------------------------------------------
- Always prefer there being an answer: enforce min matches after generate/refill.
- Text box may be used only `tries` times. Same-color triple awards +1 try.
- Same-color triple also drops a cherry bomb from the top.
- Using a bomb in an equation detonates everything in its 3×3.
- Bombs sometimes spawn in the initial board (~4%) and in refill (~5%)
  and also randomly after a non-same-color clear (12%).
- Candies never stay floating; gravity packs them down, new ones fall in.
- After 50 removals the operator changes; after all four operators the
  level increases (capped at 4 for rules) and a new addition round starts.
- Running out of time ends the game even if matches remain.
- Running out of text tries AND zero matches ends the game.

------------------------------------------------------------
19. Suggested file structure (single file is required)
------------------------------------------------------------
helpers: load_cfg, save_cfg, sdl_window, parse_start_level,
         make_tone, make_soft_boom
classes: Candy, Particle, Sparkle, Game
entry:   if __name__ == "__main__": Game(parse_start_level(sys.argv)).run()

No other assets. No tkinter (Apple Command Line Tools Tk crashes on some Macs).